# agreement-v1 — co-located policy for the D3 cross-paper agreement map (issue #64)
#
# A policy is a *versioned trusted input* (spec §4, assumption A2). The four "papers" in the
# co-located `example.lara` check against THIS single policy, so a cross-paper attack can only
# form through the *same* declared `contrary` relation — never per-artifact. This is the point of
# §7: a prose "disagreement" becomes a typed edge only if it can construct a `rebut`/`undercut`/
# `undermine` against a declaration in this file.
#
# Modeled on `examples/A/empirical-v1.policy.lara`, but at real-corpus grain: the two schemes are
# the pruning cluster's comparison family from `corpus-units/corpus-v1.policy.lara`
# (`controlled_comparison` + its `null_comparison` dual, concluding `better` / `not_better`), so
# the demo speaks the same vocabulary the frozen corpus units already use (e.g.
# `corpus-units/adaptive-pruning/C03`, whose claim is `better(apt, prune_distill_cofi, ...)`).
#
# The design decision this policy encodes (issue #64, and README.md design constraint #1):
# there is exactly ONE genuine conflict source — the `better`/`not_better` contrary pair — and it
# is a single pattern over (S, B, Q, D). It fires ONLY when two conclusions unify to the SAME
# instance, i.e. share the SAME setting index D. There is deliberately NO cross-setting contrary
# (no declaration relating `better(S,B,Q,D1)` to `not_better(S,B,Q,D2)` for D1 ≠ D2). v0.1 has no
# vocabulary to declare one: eval-settings-as-worlds is the possible-worlds follow-up, not v0.1.
# So two papers that "disagree" in prose but formalize to different settings produce ZERO attacks
# — LARA refuses to manufacture the disagreement (the demonstrated feature, not a limitation).

policy agreement-v1

# --- Inference schemes -------------------------------------------------------------------------

# A favorable head-to-head comparison presumptively supports a `better` claim (defeasible,
# observation O4), subject to critical questions. Setting index D is carried through to the
# conclusion, so the conclusion's identity includes the setting the comparison was run in.
rule controlled_comparison(S, B, Q, D, Exp)
  mode       = defeasible
  premises   = [ reports(Exp, compares(S, B, Q, D, favorable)) ]
  conclusion = better(S, B, Q, D)
  question baseline_parity      : matched_setup(S, B, Exp)   (mandatory)
  question measurement_validity : valid_measurement(Q, Exp)  (mandatory)
  question variance_reported    : variance_reported(Exp)     (mandatory)
  question scope_match          : scope_matched(S, Q, D)     (optional)

# The null / non-superiority dual. Concludes the declared contrary of `better` on the SAME
# (S, B, Q, D), so an instance of this scheme rebuts an instance of `controlled_comparison` only
# when both name the same system, baseline, metric, AND setting — this is what lets a *second
# paper* attack the first (the genuine-disagreement pair below), and what stops a second paper in
# a *different* setting from attacking it (the setting-mismatch pair).
rule null_comparison(S, B, Q, D, Exp)
  mode       = defeasible
  premises   = [ reports(Exp, compares(S, B, Q, D, null)) ]
  conclusion = not_better(S, B, Q, D)
  question baseline_parity      : matched_setup(S, B, Exp)   (mandatory)
  question measurement_validity : valid_measurement(Q, Exp)  (mandatory)
  question adequate_power       : powered(Exp)               (mandatory)

# --- Conflict ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The ONLY source of propositional conflict (spec §4, §3.2). Declared in BOTH directions on
# purpose: v0.1 `contrary` is asymmetric (spec §4.1), and `rebut` needs (concl w, concl u) to be
# a declared pair, so a mutual-rebut `contested` 2-cycle requires both orientations. Both sides
# range over the SAME (S, B, Q, D) — including the setting index D — so the pair matches only on
# full atom identity. Two conclusions that differ in D are NOT a contrary instance: no edge.
contrary better(S, B, Q, D)     not_better(S, B, Q, D)
contrary not_better(S, B, Q, D) better(S, B, Q, D)

# --- Exceptions (undercutting conditions) ------------------------------------------------------

# Each scheme carries its undercut licence (corpus-v1 convention). The demo declares no undercut
# edges — its only attacks are the genuine-disagreement rebut 2-cycle — but the licences are
# here so the policy is a faithful subset of the corpus comparison family.
exception controlled_comparison : confounded_comparison(S, B, Q, D)
exception null_comparison       : underpowered_null(S, B, Q, D)

# WELL-FORMEDNESS (spec §8.1, Path B): both schemes are defeasible, so neither `better` nor
# `not_better` is the consequent of a strict rule or reachable on a strict chain. Every
# proposition in the `contrary` pair is therefore defeasibly attackable and the policy passes the
# R12 check (as in empirical-v1/v2, strict-v1, and corpus-v1). This matters here specifically:
# the genuine-disagreement pair's headline claims ARE the contested propositions, so if either
# were strict-reachable the policy would be REJECTED at compile time — the §8.1 flip criterion.
# See the agreement-map write-up (docs/demos/d3-agreement-map.md) for the recorded decision.
